 "recommendation": {
  "status": "inferred from the measurements above; thresholds are in-sample or 2-fold cross-fitted on these sets only (a few hundred items, agent-labelled where no reference exists)",
  "same_object_merge (a)": "Keep Qwen3-VL-8B (already resident): 93.4% audited, AUROC 0.98, 0.047 s/pair at 16 in flight. Jev-Omni is 89.0% (AUROC 0.95) and adds 25 GiB. Useful only as a second vote: when both agree (90% of pairs) they are 95.5% right; disagreement -> keep apart and escalate. SigLIP cosine alone: AUROC 0.86, and the core's 0.9858 threshold calls none of these 300 pairs 'same'.",
  "naming (b)": "No closed-set chooser should name objects alone: the per-video vocabulary lacks the right word for 23% of best views and neither Jev-Omni nor Qwen ever picked 'none of these' (0/53, 1/53). Jev-Omni over the full vocabulary has the most useful confidence (AUROC 0.88): accept its pick at p >= 0.95 (about 20% of objects, 88% right cross-fitted) and send the rest to free-text VLM naming. That beats the core's SigLIP rule (38% accepted at 60% right).",
  "discovery gate (c)": "Jev-Omni judges object/part/background/group better than Qwen (66% vs 55%, whole-object-vs-rest AUROC 0.77 vs 0.70, ECE 0.12 vs 0.38; X2's generative Qwen judge 58%). Nothing reaches 90% precision cross-fitted (Jev: 78% on the 22% it is surest of). Use it as a ranking score, not a gate.",
  "EHS yes/no on frames (d)": "Neither local model is a detector at p=0.5: both say yes to under 10% of questions when 28% are yes. Their ranking works: Qwen AUROC 0.87 (0.955 on clear labels), Jev 0.74. Screening threshold for Qwen p_yes: 0.004 gives 63% recall at 10% false alarms (all items); 0.011 gives 82% recall at 6% false alarms (clear labels). Gemini's stated probability is the best balanced (83%) and best on blocked aisles (AUROC 0.88 vs Qwen 0.78 vs Jev 0.64), but it takes 10-40 s a request and is a cloud call. Jev is best on cables across the floor (AUROC 0.99) and people on platforms (1.0), with 9 and 6 positives only.",
  "fact -> rule / severity routing (e, text)": "Jev-Omni text: 99% rule, 77% severity, ECE 0.010 on rules, 0.1 s a question. Qwen text: 96% / 70%, overconfident on severity (ECE 0.24). Laya zero-shot: 42% / 48% at 30 ms per fact (two questions): not usable without fine-tuning on our rules (its card says the same). Where Jev-Omni belongs in the ladder: the text step that routes each fact to its rule and severity, accepted at p >= 0.35 for rules (99% decided, 99% right) and p >= 0.79 for severity (75% decided, 90% right in-sample, 78% / 88% cross-fitted), with the rest going to review.",
  "placement": "Jev-Omni needs 25.3 GiB resident. On the core's GPU 0 (peak 45 GiB) that makes about 70 GiB, just under the 72 GiB flag, so it is safer on the third A100 next to LingBot, or loaded only for the text-routing step. It is not a replacement for Qwen3-VL on images.",
